### Attack Surface
- File System Evidence: `int fd = open(filename, O_RDONLY);` — Candidate: `filename` parameter
  The `filename` parameter is directly used in the `open()` system call without any sanitization or validation. Since `filename` originates from user-controlled input (`argv[1]` in the `main` function), an attacker can specify arbitrary file paths (e.g., `/etc/passwd`, `../../../../etc/shadow`, `/proc/self/environ`) to read sensitive system files, leading to an arbitrary file read vulnerability and path traversal.

- Memory/Resources Evidence: `memcpy(out, buf, BUF_SIZE);` — Candidate: `memcpy` operation
  The `memcpy` function copies `BUF_SIZE` bytes from `buf` to `out`. While `buf` is null-terminated at `buf[14 + count]`, if the file content is shorter than the available space (`count < remaining`), `buf` will contain data beyond this null terminator (e.g., the `0`s or `7`s set by `memset` in the `main` function). Copying the entire `BUF_SIZE` bytes means that `out` will also contain this extraneous data. Although `printf("%s", out);` in `main` will stop at the first null terminator, the `out` buffer itself holds this additional data. This pattern can lead to information leakage if `out` is processed by other functions or transmitted over a network protocol that does not respect null terminators, exposing potentially sensitive buffer contents that are not part of the intended file data.

